<section class="reading-section sources" id="sources">
  <div class="reading-label"><p class="eyebrow">Sources & credits</p><h2>Data sources<br />and credits</h2></div>
  <div class="reading-copy">
    <p>Inspired by <a href="https://nunosempere.com/">Nuño Sempere (@NunoSempere)</a> and <a href="https://p3humansonai.substack.com/p/humans-on-ai-53-september-15th-2026">Humans on AI #53, September 15, 2026</a>.</p>
    <details class="method-details" id="population-sources"><summary>US households and taxes</summary>
      <p>US household data come from the Census Bureau's <a href="https://www.census.gov/data/datasets/2026/demo/cps/cps-asec-2026.html">2026 Current Population Survey Annual Social and Economic Supplement</a>. Income refers to 2025 and employment status to March 2026. Survey weights estimate adult citizens' population shares. The survey omits institutionalized adults, most military barracks and citizens living abroad.</p>
      <p>Social Security includes retirement and disability benefits. Benefits include cash payments, modeled net tax refunds and Census valuations of SNAP, WIC, school meals, housing and energy assistance. Medicare and Medicaid count as coverage only. Adults without earnings include retirees, students and others who are not seeking work.</p>
      <p>Tax references are computed from <a href="https://www.census.gov/topics/income-poverty/income/guidance/tax-model.html">Census tax estimates</a> and assigned to non-capital and capital income for this model. These are average effective rates. Investment income includes reported or imputed capital gains but misses some consumption funded by asset sales. Sharing household income, allocating taxes by income source and averaging within groups are model assumptions.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="method-details" id="job-search-source"><summary>Job search and background growth</summary>
      <p>The 85% job-search default comes from the <a href="https://www.bls.gov/news.release/disp.t08.htm">BLS January 2026 displaced-worker survey, table 8</a>, released August 27, 2026. Of workers aged 20 or older displaced during 2023–25, 67.6% were employed and 19.3% unemployed. Their combined 86.9% labor-force participation rounds to 85% on the slider. This is a proxy for future job search, not a measured median or a chance of finding work.</p>
      <p>Background annual growth is assumed to be 2% in the US and 3% in the rest of the world. For comparison, <a href="https://www.cbo.gov/publication/62050">CBO's February 2026 outlook</a> projects 1.8% US real growth from 2027 onward, already including a small AI productivity contribution. The <a href="https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2026/06/11/global-economic-prospects-june-2026-press-release">World Bank's June 2026 outlook</a> projects 2.8% world growth in 2027. The simulator assumes these rounded background rates and adds the selected AI contribution separately. The forecasts do not measure growth without AI.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="method-details"><summary>World GDP and trade</summary>
      <p>The World Bank's 2025 figures put <a href="https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/NY.GDP.MKTP.CD?locations=US-1W">US GDP at $30.77 trillion and the rest of the world at $87.58 trillion</a>, a ratio of 2.846. Its <a href="https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?locations=US-1W">population figures are 341.8 million and 7.874 billion</a>, a ratio of 23.037. GDP determines market size. Population has no effect on the calculation.</p>
      <p>The <a href="https://www.bea.gov/sites/default/files/2026-06/trad1326.pdf">BEA's 2025 trade figures (page 4)</a> give US imports of $4.362 trillion and exports of $3.430 trillion. Imports from the other actor are 14.18% of US GDP and 3.92% of foreign GDP. These shares exclude trade within the foreign bloc. The model balances imports and exports at their geometric mean before profit transfers.</p>
      <p>The <a href="https://hai.stanford.edu/ai-index/2026-ai-index-report/research-and-development">Stanford AI Index 2026</a> records 59 notable models from the US and 35 from China. The model allows frontier AI abroad; model counts do not determine productivity or tax responses.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="method-details"><summary>Economic research</summary>
      <ol class="source-list">
        <li><a href="https://doi.org/10.1257/jep.33.2.3">Daron Acemoglu and Pascual Restrepo, "Automation and New Tasks" (2019)</a><p>Productivity, displacement and new tasks are separate mechanisms.</p></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.elibrary.imf.org/view/journals/006/2024/002/article-A001-en.xml">IMF, "Broadening the Gains from Generative AI: The Role of Fiscal Policies" (2024)</a><p>Income support, capital taxation and international constraints on taxing mobile profits.</p></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w23806">João Guerreiro, Sergio Rebelo and Pedro Teles, "Should Robots Be Taxed?"</a><p>Tax choices depend on workers' ability to adjust. Its robot-tax results do not determine this simulator's tax rates.</p></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-employment-outlook-2018_empl_outlook-2018-en/full-report/component-8.html">OECD Employment Outlook 2018, chapter 4</a><p>Unemployment support, wage insurance and schemes that preserve employment.</p></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w16796">Ina Simonovska and Michael Waugh, "The Elasticity of Trade: Estimates and Evidence"</a><p>Informs the trade elasticity of 4 used here. This is not an AI-specific estimate.</p></li>
        <li><a href="https://economics.mit.edu/sites/default/files/publications/GT_AERversion.pdf">Costas Arkolakis, Arnaud Costinot and Andrés Rodríguez-Clare, "New Trade Models, Same Old Gains?" (2012)</a><p>Relates gains from trade to import access and substitution. The simulator uses a simpler model of consumption in two economies.</p></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.census.gov/library/working-papers/2014/adrm/ces-wp-14-03.html">Antoine Gervais and J. Bradford Jensen, "The Tradability of Services" (2014)</a><p>Examines foreign competition in services. The simulator's 40% tradable share is a separate assumption.</p></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w21906">David Autor, David Dorn and Gordon Hanson, "The China Shock: Learning from Labor Market Adjustment to Large Changes in Trade" (2016)</a><p>Documents lasting effects of import competition on local employment. The simulator's one-for-one adjustment rule is a separate assumption.</p></li>
        <li><a href="https://omohundro.files.wordpress.com/2009/03/stewart99_a_puzzle_for_pirates.pdf">Ian Stewart, "A Puzzle for Pirates" (1999)</a><p>The voting puzzle behind the analogy.</p></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.imf.org/en/Blogs/Articles/2021/11/18/sharing-the-gains-of-automation-the-role-of-fiscal-policy">IMF, "Sharing the Gains of Automation: The Role of Fiscal Policy" (2021)</a><p>Compares capital-income taxes with taxes on additional profits. AI-profit attribution and investment responses in this simulator are separate assumptions.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </details>
    <p class="source-note">Sources supply data and economic mechanisms. The voting rules and numerical responses to policy are assumptions of this simulator.</p>
  </div>
</section>
